The tracks of a primary particle passing through the TPC is reconstructed by finding ionization clusters along the track. The \(x\) and \(y\) coordinates of a cluster are determined by the charge measured on adjacent pads in a single pad row. (The local \(x\) axis is along the direction of the pad row, while the local \(y\) axis extends from the beamline outward through the middle of, and perpendicular to, the pad rows. The \(z\) axis lies along the beam line.) The \(z\) coordinate of a point inside the TPC is determined by measuring the time of drift of a cluster of secondary electrons from the point of origin to the anodes on the endcap and dividing by the average drift velocity. To minimize the variations in drift velocity, the cathode voltage are set so that the electric field in the TPC corresponds to the peak in the drift velocity curve. Typical drift velocity in STAR TPC is 5.45 cm/\(\mu\)s.

